<!--Post: 851--><!--Time: 1093134413-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST851"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Psychesknot<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Psychesknot<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 21, 2004 - 5:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Long shot, I admit, but I found this interesting. <BR> <BR>In one of his letters, BTK mentions some serial killers, including .. H. H. Holmes. <BR> <BR>In February 2004 a book hit the stands called: <BR>&#34;The Devil in the White City &#34; <BR>subtitled &#34;Murder, Magic, and Madness at the Fair that Changed America&#34; <BR> <BR>It is an interweaving of the building of the 1893 Chicago World&#39;s Fair with Holmes building a strange hotel designed for murder. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.randomhouse.com/crown/devilinthewhitecity/home.html" target=_top>http://www.randomhouse.com/crown/devilinthewhiteci ty/home.html</a> <BR> <BR>It sounds quite unique and interesting, so I&#39;ll be picking up this book soon.  My point is, this book, featuring a sort of &#34;hero&#34; of BTK&#39;s came out in Feb. 2004.  BTK started his communications again in March 2004.  I know it&#39;s a stretch, but he does seem to be a true crime reader? <BR> <BR>Here is a quote from Holmes confession: <BR> <BR>&#34;I was born with the devil in me. I could not help the fact that I was a murderer, no more than the poet can help the inspiration to sing.&#34; <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 851-->
<!--Post: 852--><!--Time: 1093139986-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST852"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 21, 2004 - 6:59 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Psychesknot makes a very good point here. I haven&#39;t read the book but the view that BTK might be a true crime reader is a worthy one. Perhaps I should have included this in my original profile except that it is a fairly common element for contemporary serial killers. Nonetheless, it&#39;s a piece of BTK&#39;s rather large and complex puzzle, and I&#39;m happy that the poster brought it up. Good observation. <BR> <BR>As to the timing of the publication of the book, that&#39;s also not unreasonable if one accepts that BTK is a true crime reader. Horrible as it is to admit, some of my most avid fan mail over the years has come from serial killers sitting on death row. So, as I said, this is a good post with some good ideas. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 852-->
<!--Post: 853--><!--Time: 1093141292-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST853"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 21, 2004 - 7:21 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The Devil in the White City By erik larson is a very good book but it was published several years ago not in 2004. I read it, almost 2 years ago. Maybe your talking about the paperback version, but any real true crime buff would want the hard-cover edition. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 853-->
<!--Post: 854--><!--Time: 1093147096-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST854"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_kelleher<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_kelleher<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 21, 2004 - 8:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That&#39;s basically correct. According to randomhouse.com the hardcover version was published in February 2003, the paperback in February 2004.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 854-->
<!--Post: 855--><!--Time: 1093149451-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST855"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Psychesknot<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Psychesknot<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, August 21, 2004 - 9:37 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks for the correction.  I see the second publishing date, most likely for the paperback version. <BR> <BR>Perhaps he&#39;s thrifty and only buys paperbacks<IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0> <BR> <BR>I have felt since hearing of the case that he was a true crime magazine reader back in his early years.  An adult might have logically progresses to true crime books. <BR> <BR>Remember also an article came out in January that a book on BTK was in the works.  I think that also could have sparked him to begin communicating out of the blue.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 855-->
<!--Post: 861--><!--Time: 1093237645-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST861"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:zodiacfiles@aol.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Ed_neil<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ed_neil<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, August 22, 2004 - 10:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I also posted several possible reasons concerning the timing of BTK&#39;s return in the <a href="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/messages/11/38.html?1092700111" target="_blank">BTK:Zodiac Buff</a> thread.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 861-->
<!--Post: 863--><!--Time: 1093238276-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST863"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scbenson2001<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scbenson2001<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, August 22, 2004 - 10:17 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think the capture of gary ridgeway, the green river killer and the subquent press he recieved had alot to do with btk&#39;s resurfacing. Green River was also thought to be gone, dead or in jail on another offence but was revealed to be around the whole time and still killing, although and a much reduced rate. Btk seems to be responding. &#34;Me too&#34; .<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 863-->
<!--Post: 894--><!--Time: 1093411852-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST894"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Psychesknot<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Psychesknot<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, August 24, 2004 - 10:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I just have the feeling BTK reads lots of true crime.  Starting with the old True Crime, True Detective mags of his teen era, the ones with the lurid cover pics.  Then refining it to true crime books later.  I think he would like to see some truth and detail in any book about him and this may be why he&#39;s resurfaced. <BR> <BR>I ordered The Devil in the White City in any case... sounds like a good read to me.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 894-->
